# 164.5 plan 06 — pg-lane proof log for the DRIFT-04 removal migration

Dated 2026-09-07. Subject:
`supabase/migrations/20260908120000_drop_create_allocator_connected_strategy.sql`.

Substrate: `scripts/pg-lane/run.sh` — a throwaway local PostgreSQL cluster that
`initdb`s fresh, listens on 127.0.0.1 only, and is destroyed by its own EXIT
trap. **Never shared TEST. Never PROD.** Nothing in this log touched a hosted
database, and no `supabase db push`, `db reset`, `--project-ref` or `--db-url`
was run.

Exit codes: **0, 3, 3, 3, 0**.

⭐ **A THIRD ROUND WAS APPENDED 2026-09-07 — proofs F, F2a and F2b, exit codes
3, 0, 0.** It is at the END of this file, under its own heading. It exists
because the review found that the migration header's sentence at its lines 78-81
("without it, a dependent aborts the apply and names itself") had NO lane proof
(MED-4), and that the pre-flight's assertion (b) claimed a completeness pg_depend
cannot give (MED-1). Everything between here and that heading is the dated record
of rounds one and two and was NOT re-run; the counts and exit codes in this
paragraph and the next are theirs, not the file's total.

⚠️ **This log is the SECOND round.** A first round of proofs A–D was taken
against the migration *before* the review pass added its `BEGIN;` /
`SET lock_timeout` / `COMMIT;` wrapper, and read 0, 3, 3, 3. It is superseded by
what follows: proof E exists only because that wrapper was added, and the
`rtrim()` in check 2's message is visible in proof C's output here (`argument
counts: 2.`) where the earlier round printed a trailing space.

## What the five proofs establish

| Proof | Setup | Expected | Observed |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A** | a stand-in at the exact 11-argument signature, SECURITY DEFINER, with BOTH the `authenticated` and `service_role` grants | migration applies, zero overloads survive | `GATE PASSED`, exit **0** |
| **B** | no stand-in — the function is simply not there | STEP 1 ERRORS, because it carries no existence-tolerant modifier | `ERROR: 42883: function … does not exist`, exit **3** |
| **C** | stand-in PLUS a second overload of a different arity that STEP 1 does not name | check 2 of the verify block RAISEs and names the surviving arity | `P0001: … 1 overload(s) … survive … at argument counts: 2.`, exit **3** |
| **D** | a MUTANT copy of the migration whose STEP 1 is given the existence-tolerant modifier AND a wrong 3-type signature | STEP 1 silently no-ops; check 1 of the verify block RAISEs | `NOTICE: … does not exist, skipping` then `P0001: … still has 1 overload(s) at 11 arguments after STEP 1`, exit **3** |
| **E** | the same setup as C, but the REAL file is run through `\i` with `ON_ERROR_STOP` relaxed so the session survives the RAISE and can look at the database afterwards | the transaction wrapper ROLLS STEP 1 BACK, so the 11-argument overload is still there | `BEGIN / SET / DROP FUNCTION / ERROR P0001 / ROLLBACK` then `ATOMICITY PROVEN … (count=1)`, exit **0** |

**Proof D is load-bearing.** It reproduces, on a real cluster, the exact vacuous
pass the plan's D-01 refuses: the removal statement runs, PostgreSQL prints
`does not exist, skipping`, the migration would have reported success — and the
inverted `DO $verify$` block catches it. That is the falsification that makes
the verify block a test rather than a decoration.

**Proof B is the second.** It is the direct measurement that STEP 1 is NOT
existence-tolerant: aimed at a signature the database does not hold, it errors
and names the function rather than succeeding over nothing.

**Proof E is the third**, and it exists because a review finding created the
claim it checks. Adding `BEGIN;` / `COMMIT;` asserts that a RAISE in STEP 2
leaves nothing behind. Asserting it is not measuring it — so E runs the real,
unmodified file and then LOOKS: the removal is rolled back and the overload is
still present. Without the wrapper this proof fails, which is what makes it a
test of the wrapper rather than of PostgreSQL.

The real migration file was never edited to produce D. The mutant is a `sed`
rewrite into a temporary copy; the `diff` between them is printed in the run
output below and is exactly two lines.

## Driver and fixtures (verbatim)

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Lane proofs for supabase/migrations/20260908120000_drop_create_allocator_connected_strategy.sql
# Substrate: scripts/pg-lane/run.sh — a throwaway local cluster. Never TEST, never PROD.
# Run from the repository root.
set -u

MIG="supabase/migrations/20260908120000_drop_create_allocator_connected_strategy.sql"
F="$(mktemp -d)"
echo "fixtures: $F"
echo

cat > "$F/standin-one.sql" <<'SQL'
-- Stand-in reproducing PROD's object as the pre-flight measures it: the exact
-- 11-argument signature, SECURITY DEFINER, and BOTH grants. Body is trivial —
-- the migration asserts SHAPE, not behaviour.
DO $roles$ BEGIN
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'authenticated') THEN CREATE ROLE authenticated NOLOGIN; END IF;
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'service_role')  THEN CREATE ROLE service_role  NOLOGIN; END IF;
END $roles$;
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.create_allocator_connected_strategy(
  p_user_id uuid, p_portfolio_id uuid, p_exchange text, p_label text,
  p_strategy_name text, p_api_key_encrypted text, p_api_secret_encrypted text,
  p_passphrase_encrypted text, p_dek_encrypted text, p_nonce text,
  p_kek_version integer
) RETURNS TABLE(strategy_id uuid, api_key_id uuid)
  LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER
  SET search_path TO 'public', 'pg_catalog'
  AS $standin$ BEGIN RETURN; END; $standin$;
REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.create_allocator_connected_strategy(uuid, uuid, text, text, text, text, text, text, text, text, integer) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT ALL ON FUNCTION public.create_allocator_connected_strategy(uuid, uuid, text, text, text, text, text, text, text, text, integer) TO authenticated;
GRANT ALL ON FUNCTION public.create_allocator_connected_strategy(uuid, uuid, text, text, text, text, text, text, text, text, integer) TO service_role;
SQL

cat > "$F/standin-extra-overload.sql" <<'SQL'
-- A SECOND overload of a DIFFERENT arity, which STEP 1 does not name.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.create_allocator_connected_strategy(p_a uuid, p_b text)
  RETURNS void LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER AS $standin2$ BEGIN END; $standin2$;
SQL

cat > "$F/gate-absent.sql" <<'SQL'
DO $gate$
DECLARE v_n INTEGER;
BEGIN
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_n FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
   WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.proname='create_allocator_connected_strategy';
  IF v_n <> 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'GATE FAILED: % overload(s) of create_allocator_connected_strategy survive', v_n;
  END IF;
  RAISE NOTICE 'GATE PASSED: zero overloads named create_allocator_connected_strategy remain';
END;
$gate$;
SQL

# Proof D's mutant: STEP 1 given the existence-tolerant modifier AND a wrong
# signature, so the removal matches nothing and silently succeeds. Built by
# rewriting the real file into a COPY; the real file is never edited.
sed -e 's/^DROP FUNCTION public\.create_allocator_connected_strategy($/DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS public.create_allocator_connected_strategy(/' \
    -e 's/^  uuid, uuid, text, text, text, text, text, text, text, text, integer$/  uuid, uuid, text/' \
    "$MIG" > "$F/mutant.sql"

# Proof E runs the REAL, UNMODIFIED migration through psql's \i with
# ON_ERROR_STOP relaxed, so the run SURVIVES the RAISE and can then look at the
# database. That is the only way to observe what STEP 2's failure LEAVES BEHIND.
MIG_ABS="$PWD/$MIG"
cat > "$F/proof-e.sql" <<SQL
\\set ON_ERROR_STOP 0
\\i $MIG_ABS
\\set ON_ERROR_STOP 1
DO \$probe\$
DECLARE v_n INTEGER;
BEGIN
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_n FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
   WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.proname='create_allocator_connected_strategy' AND p.pronargs = 11;
  IF v_n <> 1 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'ATOMICITY FAILED: STEP 2 raised, but the 11-argument overload did NOT come back (count=%). STEP 1 committed behind a failed migration', v_n;
  END IF;
  RAISE NOTICE 'ATOMICITY PROVEN: STEP 2 raised and STEP 1 was ROLLED BACK — the 11-argument overload is still present (count=%)', v_n;
END;
\$probe\$;
SQL

echo "MUTANT diff (proof D) — the two lines the mutation changed:"
diff "$MIG" "$F/mutant.sql"
echo

run() {
  local label="$1"; shift
  local wd; wd="$(mktemp -d)"
  echo "================================================================"
  echo "$label"
  echo "================================================================"
  bash scripts/pg-lane/run.sh --workdir "$wd" "$@" 2>&1 | tail -n 18
  local rc=${PIPESTATUS[0]}
  echo "LANE EXIT=$rc"
  echo
  rm -rf "$wd"
}

run "PROOF A (GREEN) — stand-in present, real migration applies, gate finds zero overloads" \
    --apply "$F/standin-one.sql" "$MIG" --gate "$F/gate-absent.sql"

run "PROOF B (RED) — NO stand-in: STEP 1 must ERROR because it carries no existence-tolerant modifier" \
    --apply "$MIG" --gate "$F/gate-absent.sql"

run "PROOF C (RED) — a second overload of a different arity survives STEP 1; check 2 of the verify block must RAISE" \
    --apply "$F/standin-one.sql" "$F/standin-extra-overload.sql" "$MIG" --gate "$F/gate-absent.sql"

run "PROOF D (RED) — MUTANT with the existence-tolerant modifier and a wrong signature: STEP 1 no-ops, check 1 of the verify block must RAISE" \
    --apply "$F/standin-one.sql" "$F/mutant.sql" --gate "$F/gate-absent.sql"

run "PROOF E (ATOMICITY) — a second overload makes STEP 2 RAISE; the transaction wrapper must ROLL STEP 1 BACK, leaving the 11-argument overload present" \
    --apply "$F/standin-one.sql" "$F/standin-extra-overload.sql" --gate "$F/proof-e.sql"

rm -rf "$F"
```

