# THIRD ROUND — proofs F, F2a and F2b (added 2026-09-07, review MED-4 and MED-1)

Everything above stays as the dated record of rounds one and two. This section is
an **appendix**, not a replacement: no proof above was re-run, none of their exit
codes changed, and the subject migration is byte-identical to the file they used.

Exit codes, this round: **3, 0, 0**.

## Why this round exists

**MED-4.** The migration header asserts, at its lines 78-81, that without the
dependent-removing modifier *"a dependent aborts the apply and names itself"*.
Rounds one and two proved four other claims and **left that one unmeasured**.
Asserting is not measuring — the same standard proof E was added under, applied
to the one remaining unmeasured sentence in the header.

**MED-1.** The pre-flight's assertion (b) read `pg_depend` and printed
*"ZERO — nothing in the catalogue depends on this overload"*. That is a
completeness claim `pg_depend` cannot support: a plpgsql body that CALLS a
function records **no** `pg_depend` row — the call is resolved out of the body
text at execution time. F2a and F2b measure that hole on a real cluster, which is
what makes the new `(b.2)` `prosrc` reader in
`scripts/preflight-drop-allocator-fn.sh` load-bearing rather than belt-and-braces.

## What the three proofs establish

| Proof | Setup | Expected | Observed |
|---|---|---|---|
| **F** | the same stand-in as A/C/E, PLUS a **view** whose definition calls the function — a real `pg_depend` dependent | STEP 1 ABORTS and NAMES the dependent, because it carries no dependent-removing modifier | `ERROR: 2BP01: cannot drop function … because other objects depend on it` / `DETAIL: view v_alloc_dependent depends on function …`, exit **3** |
| **F2a** | the stand-in PLUS a plpgsql function whose body calls it. **No migration is applied.** | `pg_depend` reports **0** while `prosrc` reports **1** — the blindness itself, measured before anything is removed | `BLINDNESS MEASURED: pg_depend rows = 0 while prosrc naming bodies = 1`, exit **0** |
| **F2b** | the same setup, then the REAL migration | the bare removal **SUCCEEDS** (nothing aborts it), and calling the surviving caller raises `42883` | `RUNTIME BREAK MEASURED: … raises 42883 undefined_function` then `BLINDSPOT PROVEN: …`, exit **0** |

**Proof F closes MED-4 directly.** It is the measurement behind the header
sentence at lines 78-81. PostgreSQL's own `DETAIL:` line names the dependent
(`view v_alloc_dependent`), so "names itself" is observed, not inferred — and its
`HINT:` names the dependent-removing modifier the file deliberately refuses,
which is the clearest possible statement of what refusing it buys.

**Proofs F2a and F2b are the MED-1 pair, and F2b's exit 0 is the point.**
Every other RED proof in this log exits 3. F2b exits **0** because the removal
*worked* — there was nothing in the catalogue to stop it. That is the hole:

* `pg_depend` reports zero (F2a measures this on the pre-removal database, so the
  zero cannot be an artefact of the function having gone away);
* the bare removal therefore succeeds, silently, on the auto-apply-to-PROD route;
* `public.calls_the_allocator_fn()` then raises `42883` when called — **measured
  by calling it**, inside an `EXCEPTION WHEN undefined_function` arm that RAISEs
  if the call unexpectedly succeeds, so the gate cannot pass vacuously.

⛔ **F2b is a proof of a GAP, not of a guard.** It shows what the migration does
NOT catch. The thing that catches it is `(b.2)` of the pre-flight, whose own red
arms are in that script's `--self-test`. Nothing in the migration was changed in
response — adding the dependent-removing modifier would make F's abort disappear,
which is the opposite of what is wanted.

## Driver (verbatim, new parts only)

The `standin-one.sql` fixture, the `gate-absent.sql` fixture and the `run()`
helper are **byte-identical** to round two's, reproduced above; only the new
fixtures, the new gates and the three `run` invocations follow.

```bash
# A REAL catalogue dependent: a view whose definition calls the function. This is
# the class pg_depend DOES record, i.e. exactly what the pre-flight's (b.1)
# measures and what the header claims will abort the apply.
cat > "$F/dependent-view.sql" <<'SQL'
CREATE VIEW public.v_alloc_dependent AS
  SELECT strategy_id, api_key_id
    FROM public.create_allocator_connected_strategy(
      NULL::uuid, NULL::uuid, NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::text,
      NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::integer);
SQL

# F2's contrast fixture: a plpgsql CALLER, which records NO pg_depend row.
cat > "$F/plpgsql-caller.sql" <<'SQL'
CREATE FUNCTION public.calls_the_allocator_fn() RETURNS void
  LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $caller$
BEGIN
  PERFORM * FROM public.create_allocator_connected_strategy(
    NULL::uuid, NULL::uuid, NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::text,
    NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::text, NULL::integer);
END;
$caller$;
SQL

# F2a's gate, run on the PRE-removal database, so the zero below is not an
# artefact of the function having gone away.
cat > "$F/gate-predrop-depend-zero.sql" <<'SQL'
DO $gate$
DECLARE v_dep INTEGER; v_src INTEGER;
BEGIN
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_dep FROM pg_depend d
   WHERE d.refclassid = 'pg_proc'::regclass
     AND d.refobjid = (SELECT p.oid FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
                        WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.proname='create_allocator_connected_strategy')
     AND d.deptype <> 'i' AND d.classid <> 0;
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_src FROM pg_proc p
   WHERE p.prosrc ILIKE '%create_allocator_connected_strategy%'
     AND p.proname <> 'create_allocator_connected_strategy';
  IF v_dep <> 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'MEASUREMENT FAILED: pg_depend reported % rows for a plpgsql caller; the blindness this proof asserts does not exist', v_dep;
  END IF;
  IF v_src <> 1 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'MEASUREMENT FAILED: prosrc reported % naming bodies, expected 1', v_src;
  END IF;
  RAISE NOTICE 'BLINDNESS MEASURED: pg_depend rows = % while prosrc naming bodies = %. A plpgsql caller is INVISIBLE to (b.1).', v_dep, v_src;
END;
$gate$;
SQL

# F2b's gate: the removal has run. Assert it SUCCEEDED (that is the hole), then
# MEASURE the runtime break by calling the survivor.
cat > "$F/gate-plpgsql-blindspot.sql" <<'SQL'
DO $gate$
DECLARE
  v_dep      INTEGER;
  v_src      INTEGER;
  v_survivor INTEGER;
BEGIN
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_src FROM pg_proc p
   WHERE p.prosrc ILIKE '%create_allocator_connected_strategy%'
     AND p.proname <> 'create_allocator_connected_strategy';
  IF v_src <> 1 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'SETUP FAILED: expected exactly 1 plpgsql body naming the function, found %', v_src;
  END IF;

  SELECT count(*) INTO v_survivor FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
   WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.proname='create_allocator_connected_strategy';
  IF v_survivor <> 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'BLINDSPOT NOT REPRODUCED: the removal did not succeed (% overload(s) survive)', v_survivor;
  END IF;

  -- ⛔ The runtime break is MEASURED, not asserted. Calling the surviving caller
  --    must raise 42883 (undefined_function). A gate that merely stated "the
  --    caller is now broken" would be a claim, not a test.
  BEGIN
    PERFORM public.calls_the_allocator_fn();
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'BLINDSPOT NOT PROVEN: the caller still RAN after the removal. The runtime break this proof asserts did not happen';
  EXCEPTION
    WHEN undefined_function THEN
      RAISE NOTICE 'RUNTIME BREAK MEASURED: calling public.calls_the_allocator_fn() after the removal raises 42883 undefined_function';
  END;

  RAISE NOTICE 'BLINDSPOT PROVEN: pg_depend recorded NOTHING for the plpgsql caller, the bare removal SUCCEEDED, and public.calls_the_allocator_fn() now raises 42883 when called. prosrc still names the function in % body/bodies. This is the class (b.2) of the pre-flight measures and pg_depend cannot.', v_src;
END;
$gate$;
SQL

run "PROOF F (RED) — a CATALOGUE dependent (a view over the function) must ABORT the apply and NAME ITSELF" \
    --apply "$F/standin-one.sql" "$F/dependent-view.sql" "$MIG" --gate "$F/gate-absent.sql"

run "PROOF F2a (MEASUREMENT) — a plpgsql caller records ZERO pg_depend rows while prosrc names it: the blindness itself" \
    --apply "$F/standin-one.sql" "$F/plpgsql-caller.sql" --gate "$F/gate-predrop-depend-zero.sql"

run "PROOF F2b (THE HOLE) — with only a plpgsql caller, the bare removal SUCCEEDS and the caller breaks at runtime" \
    --apply "$F/standin-one.sql" "$F/plpgsql-caller.sql" "$MIG" --gate "$F/gate-plpgsql-blindspot.sql"
```

